window.docContents[138].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization--Fixed-Point-Methods-for-Semilinear-Problems--Compactness-and-Monotonicity-Beyond-Contractions":{"contents":"Schauder's fixed-point theorem\n replaces metric contraction by continuity,\ncompactness, and invariance of a convex set. It can treat maps with no\nsmall-Lipschitz estimate, though it does not generally give uniqueness or a\nconvergent Picard iteration. Minty and Browder developed another direction:\nmonotonicity and coercivity of a nonlinear operator can replace compactness in\nexistence arguments \n. Schauder's theorem and Schaefer's theorem\nare proved for compact maps on real normed spaces. The validation argument below\nuses Schauder in a weaker topology to obtain existence near an approximate solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Fixed Points and Convex Regularization\u0009Fixed-Point Methods for Semilinear Problems","header":"9.5.1.2. Compactness and Monotonicity Beyond Contractions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization--Fixed-Point-Methods-for-Semilinear-Problems--Compactness-and-Monotonicity-Beyond-Contractions"}});